{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"property-descriptors","title":"Property Descriptors, freeze & seal","group":"Arrays & Objects","order":25,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every property has hidden attributes — writable, enumerable, configurable — that control whether it can change, show up in loops, or be deleted.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Inspecting descriptors</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const obj = { visible: 1 };\nconsole.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(obj, \"visible\"));\nObject.defineProperty(obj, \"secret\", { value: 42 });   // attributes default to false!\nconsole.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(obj, \"secret\"));\nconsole.log(obj, Object.keys(obj), obj.secret);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{ value: 1, writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true }\n{ value: 42, writable: false, enumerable: false, configurable: false }\n{ visible: 1 } [ 'visible' ] 42</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>When <code>false</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>writable</code></td><td>The value cannot be reassigned (throws in strict mode)</td></tr><tr><td><code>enumerable</code></td><td>Hidden from <code>for…in</code>, <code>Object.keys</code>, spread and <code>JSON.stringify</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>configurable</code></td><td>Cannot be deleted, and its attributes cannot be changed (except writable → false)</td></tr><tr><td><code>get</code> / <code>set</code></td><td>Accessor property instead of a data property (no <code>value</code>/<code>writable</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const account = {};\nObject.defineProperty(account, \"id\", { value: \"AC-1\", enumerable: true });\ntry {\n  account.id = \"hacked\";\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);\n}\ntry {\n  delete account.id;\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);\n}\nconsole.log(account.id);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Cannot assign to read only property 'id' of object '#&lt;Object&gt;'\nCannot delete property 'id' of #&lt;Object&gt;\nAC-1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-square\"></i>preventExtensions, seal, freeze</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Add props</th><th>Delete props</th><th>Change values</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Object.preventExtensions</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>Object.isExtensible</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Object.seal</code></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>Object.isSealed</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Object.freeze</code></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><code>Object.isFrozen</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const sealed = Object.seal({ a: 1 });\nsealed.a = 2;\ntry { sealed.b = 3; } catch (e) { console.log(\"seal:\", e.message); }\n\nconst frozen = Object.freeze({ a: 1, nested: { b: 1 } });\ntry { frozen.a = 2; } catch (e) { console.log(\"freeze:\", e.message); }\nfrozen.nested.b = 99;                 // freeze is SHALLOW\nconsole.log(sealed, frozen, Object.isFrozen(frozen.nested));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>seal: Cannot add property b, object is not extensible\nfreeze: Cannot assign to read only property 'a' of object '#&lt;Object&gt;'\n{ a: 2 } { a: 1, nested: { b: 99 } } false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>In sloppy mode these writes fail <strong>silently</strong>; in strict mode (modules, classes) they throw a <code>TypeError</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-snowflake\"></i>Deep freeze</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function deepFreeze(obj) {\n  for (const value of Object.values(obj)) {\n    if (value &amp;&amp; typeof value === \"object\") deepFreeze(value);\n  }\n  return Object.freeze(obj);\n}\nconst config = deepFreeze({ db: { host: \"localhost\", ports: [5432] } });\ntry {\n  config.db.ports.push(1);\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Cannot add property 1, object is not extensible</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between Object.freeze and const?</summary><div><p><code>const</code> prevents reassigning the <em>variable</em>; the object stays mutable. <code>Object.freeze</code> makes the <em>object's</em> own properties read-only and non-extensible — but only one level deep, and it says nothing about the variable.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>seal vs freeze?</summary><div><p>Both prevent adding and deleting properties. <code>seal</code> still allows changing existing values; <code>freeze</code> also makes every existing data property read-only.</p></div></details></section>"}